GROUT-FRR(7)

# NAME

grout-frr - FRR dataplane plugin for grout

# DESCRIPTION

The *dplane_grout* zebra module replaces the Linux kernel as the forwarding
plane for FRR (Free Range Routing). Once loaded, all routing decisions made by
FRR daemons (_bgpd_, _ospfd_, _ospf6d_, _isisd_, ...) are programmed directly
into grout instead of the kernel networking stack.

The plugin maintains multiple connections to the grout API socket:

- A control channel for pushing route, nexthop, address, MAC and VTEP updates.
- An event channel for receiving interface and address notifications from grout.
- A separate event channel for receiving route and nexthop notifications.

At startup (or after a grout restart), the plugin performs a full synchronization
of all grout interfaces, addresses, FDB entries, nexthops and routes into zebra's
RIB. During normal operation, updates flow bidirectionally: FRR pushes learned
routes to grout, and grout notifies FRR of link and address changes.

## Supported operations

The following zebra dataplane operations are handled:

- IPv4/IPv6 route install, update and delete
- Nexthop install, update and delete
- IP address install and uninstall
- MAC/FDB install and delete
- VXLAN flood VTEP add and delete
- Neighbor install, update and delete (used for EVPN RMAC caching)
- SRv6 encap source address

When built against the SRv6 L2 EVPN fork (see below), the plugin also handles
the three dataplane operations that fork adds for SRv6 L2 services: bridge-port
flags, bridge VLAN membership and SRv6 tunnel SID programming.

## EVPN support

The plugin supports EVPN with VXLAN overlays, including L2 VNI (type-2 MAC/IP
and type-3 flood routes), L3 VNI (type-5 IP prefix routes with symmetric IRB)
and mixed L2+L3 VNI coexistence within the same VRF.

In grout, L3 VNI VXLAN interfaces use a "VRF mode" that does not require
a bridge and SVI as Linux does. The plugin takes care of presenting VXLAN
interfaces to FRR in the way its EVPN code expects.

When a VXLAN interface is attached to a bridge domain (L2 VNI), the plugin
automatically disables dynamic FDB learning on it. In an EVPN deployment, BGP
is the authoritative source of MAC-to-VTEP reachability (type-2 routes), so
data-plane learning over the overlay is redundant and could install conflicting
or stale entries. This mirrors the _nolearning_ and _bridge_slave learning off_
settings used on Linux VXLAN interfaces in EVPN setups. The setting can be
overridden manually with *grcli interface set vxlan NAME learning on*, but the
plugin disables it again whenever it re-synchronizes the interface. Learning is
not involved for L3 VNI (VRF mode) interfaces, where reachability is driven
entirely by routes.

## SRv6 L2 EVPN support

The plugin supports the SRv6 L2 EVPN (RFC 9252) control plane of the FRR fork
carrying FRRouting PR #22570 (_SathishAviz/SRv6-L2-EVPN_, branch
_srv6-l2-evpn-support_). That fork adds EVPN E-LAN (Type-2/Type-3) and EVPN
VPWS (Type-1) over an SRv6 dataplane, decoupled from VXLAN.

Since the operations and ZAPI messages involved do not exist in earlier FRR
releases, the plugin code using them is gated on the FRR version (10.9),
like the other version-specific features. Building against the fork is
opt-in at build time: configure grout with *-Dfrr_version=srv6-l2evpn*,
which selects the fork as the FRR subproject.

The fork follows an operator-owned interface model: FRR never creates the
_SRv6 L2_ tunnel ports. The operator pre-creates them, enslaves them to the
EVPN bridge, and zebra discovers them by name: _sr6-\<n\>_ (unicast, End.DT2U
role), _bum-sr6-\<n\>_ (BUM, End.DT2M role) and _vpws-sr6-\<name\>_ (EVPN VPWS,
End.DX2 role). Zebra then reprograms them in place through three new dataplane
operations, which the plugin translates to grout.

The grout counterpart of those tunnel ports is the _sr6_ interface type
(_grcli interface add sr6 NAME domain BRIDGE_): a bridge member whose frames
are SRv6-encapsulated (H.Encaps.L2 or H.Encaps.L2.Red) toward its configured
remote SID, with a per-port flood flag selecting the unicast (known-unicast
only, from FDB entries) or BUM (eligible to bridge flooding) role. Frames
decapsulated at the local End.DT2U/DT2M SIDs are injected into the bridge
through the port referenced by the SID, so bridge learning, flooding and
split horizon see it as the ingress interface: remote MACs are never learned
from it, floods never go back out of it, and a frame decapsulated from a
remote PE is never re-encapsulated toward another one (the equivalent of the
Linux _isolated_ bridge-port flag).

BUM replication is PE-local: each PE only encapsulates toward the remote SIDs
configured on its own SRv6 ports, and with the current one-remote-SID-per-port
model an EVI spans two PEs. This differs from VXLAN EVPN head-end replication,
where every PE replicates to every remote VTEP itself; supporting more than
two PEs requires one SRv6 port per remote on each PE (or per-MAC remote SIDs,
as the Linux srl2 driver does with its per-FDB NDA_DST).

- *DPLANE_OP_BRPORT_FLAGS* pushes an EVPN bridge-port profile onto a tunnel
  port: never learn (decapsulated frames carry remote MACs), flood only in the
  BUM role, always isolated (split horizon).
- *DPLANE_OP_BRIDGE_VLAN_ADD* binds the EVI VLAN onto a tunnel port.
- *DPLANE_OP_SR6_UPDATE_SID* sets the encapsulation segment list of a tunnel
  port (the remote PE SID; the all-zero SID resets the port).

The seg6local local SID routes deserve a special mention. Upstream zebra
installs a route's nexthop group with a separate
*DPLANE_OP_NH_INSTALL* operation before the route itself. The fork changes
this for SRv6 local SIDs: the Linux kernel rejects _LWTUNNEL_ENCAP_SEG6_LOCAL_
attributes inside nexthop-group objects (RTM_NEWNEXTHOP answers EINVAL), so
zebra_nhg_install_kernel() short-circuits NHEs whose nexthop carries a
seg6local action. It marks them *NEXTHOP_GROUP_INSTALLED* without enqueueing
anything to the dataplane, and the route encoder falls back to inline
(per-route) nexthop encoding instead of RTA_NH_ID.

For a dataplane plugin this means a seg6local route arrives with an
*NEXTHOP_GROUP_INSTALLED* nexthop the plugin has never seen: the route install
references a nexthop that does not exist in the forwarding plane yet, and
every SRv6 local SID (End, End.X, End.DT4/DT6/DT46 and the new End.DX2/DT2U/
DT2M) would fail to install with ENOENT.

The plugin therefore detects inline seg6local nexthops in the route install
path (the nexthop group carried by the route dataplane context) and programs
the nexthop on the fly, right before the route that references it. The
nexthop delete path is unchanged: the fork still enqueues
*DPLANE_OP_NH_DELETE* for those NHEs since they are marked installed.

# CONTROL PLANE REPRESENTORS

For each grout interface (port, VLAN, bond, bridge, VXLAN), grout creates
a TAP device in the host network namespace with the same name. These control
plane representors allow FRR daemons to bind sockets (e.g. OSPF multicast,
BFD sessions) and exchange packets with the network through grout.

TAP devices operate at L2 (full Ethernet frames). They are configured as
NOARP since ARP/ND resolution is handled by grout, not the kernel.

For each VRF, grout creates a TUN device (named after the VRF for the default
VRF, or _gr-loopN_ for non-default VRFs). TUN devices operate at L3 (raw IP
packets) and act as the catch-all for traffic that does not match a specific
TAP. For non-default VRFs, grout also creates a kernel VRF device and enslaves
both the TUN and all TAP devices belonging to that VRF.

## Routing

IP addresses configured on grout interfaces are mirrored onto the corresponding
TAP or TUN devices as host routes (/32 for IPv4, /128 for IPv6). No connected
subnet routes are installed.

A default route pointing at the TUN device is installed in each VRF's routing
table so that all traffic originated by FRR daemons is funneled into grout for
forwarding.

For TAP devices, grout installs per-device policy routing rules
(_ip rule add oif <tap> lookup 999_) with default routes in table 999. This
ensures that _SO_BINDTODEVICE_ sockets (used by BFD, OSPF, etc.) are routed
through the correct TAP even though no connected subnet routes exist.

## Packet flow

Packets from FRR daemons follow the kernel routing table to either the TUN
(via the VRF default route) or a TAP (via policy routing). Grout reads these
packets from the TUN/TAP file descriptors and injects them into the datapath
for forwarding out the physical ports.

In the other direction, packets arriving on DPDK ports that are destined to
a local address are delivered to the appropriate TAP or TUN device, where the
kernel hands them to the FRR daemon's socket.

# SETUP

## Building the plugin

The plugin is built as a shared library (_dplane_grout.so_) and installed
into FRR's module directory. It requires FRR >= 10.5. The grout build system
handles this automatically via meson.

## Network namespace

FRR daemons must run in the same network namespace as grout so they can access
the control plane representors (TAP/TUN devices) created by grout. The
_grout.conf_ systemd drop-in for _frr.service_ is installed automatically and
takes care of this.

## Loading the plugin

Edit _/etc/frr/daemons_ and add *-M dplane_grout* to zebra's options:

```
zebra_options="  -A 127.0.0.1 -s 90000000 -M dplane_grout"
```

No other plugin-specific configuration is needed. Routing protocol
configuration (_bgpd_, _ospfd_, etc.) follows standard FRR conventions.

## Socket path

The plugin connects to grout via the path given by the *GROUT_SOCK_PATH*
environment variable, defaulting to _/run/grout.sock_.

## Startup order

Grout should be running before FRR starts. If it is not, the plugin retries
the connection every second until grout becomes available. Once connected,
the plugin performs a full synchronization before FRR begins normal operation.

# CAVEATS

## VRF backend

Only *vrf backend lite* is supported. The plugin refuses to start if
*vrf backend netns* is configured and exits immediately.

## BGP update-source

Because addresses are installed as host routes on the control plane
representors, the kernel cannot automatically pick the correct source address
for outgoing TCP sessions. BGP neighbors must be configured with an explicit
*update-source* pointing to the grout local IP:

```
router bgp 64512
    neighbor 172.16.0.2 remote-as 64512
    neighbor 172.16.0.2 update-source 172.16.0.1
exit
```

This is not needed for protocols that use multicast or link-local addresses
(OSPF, OSPFv3) since they bind to the interface directly.

## Source routes

Source-address-based routing (_from_ prefix) is not supported by grout.
The plugin logs an error and skips such routes.

## SRv6 L2 EVPN datapath

The SRv6 L2 EVPN control plane integration requires the fork (see above); it
cannot be built against upstream FRR. The L2 service dataplane is implemented
in the grout SRv6 module: the _sr6_ interface type (see the _SRv6 L2 EVPN
support_ section above), the End.DX2/End.DT2U/End.DT2M local SID behaviors
and the L2 encapsulation on the SRv6 bridge ports. It is covered by the
_srv6_l2vpn_test.sh_ datapath test and, for the control plane and the
dataplane, by three smoke tests: _srv6_l2_evpn_frr_test.sh_ (BGP EVPN
convergence between grout and a standalone FRR peer),
_srv6_l2_evpn_dataplane_frr_test.sh_ (the peer PE dataplane is a scapy
script bridging the service SIDs negotiated over BGP) and
_srv6_l2_evpn_dual_frr_test.sh_ (the peer PE runs a second grout instance
cabled at L2, exercising the datapath in both directions).

## Graceful restart

The plugin supports FRR graceful restart (*-K* sweep timer). After a zebra
crash and restart, stale routes are preserved during the sweep window and
cleaned up normally once the timer expires.

When grout itself restarts, the plugin detects the disconnection, tears down
all synchronized state, and performs a fresh full synchronization once grout
is available again. During that window the SRv6 L2 EVPN dataplane operations
targeting not yet re-mapped interfaces either fail with a transient error or
succeed without programming; in both cases the programming (the port profile,
the remote SID and the EVI VLAN) is re-issued when the interface is re-added
on reconnect or on the next EVPN realize, so the synchronization converges.

